imap-sample
-----------
connect to Gmail IMAP server and fetches the messages of INBOX to a folder "download"

syntax: imap-sample mygmailaccount@gmail.com mypassword


activesync-ms-device-oauth.py
-----------------------------
gets a Microsoft OAuth access token with the device-code flow for local
ActiveSync testing.

By default the helper reads optional local config from
tests/activesync-ms-oauth.local.json. It only saves tokens when
--token-json, --json-file, or --token-file is provided. The local config file
is ignored by git and can contain reusable values such as:

  {
    "client_id": "...",
    "tenant": "consumers",
    "servertype": "personal-ms",
    "login": "email@outlook.com",
    "activesync_url": "https://eas.outlook.com/Microsoft-Server-ActiveSync",
    "token_json": "/tmp/libetpan-eas-token.json",
    "state_file": "/tmp/libetpan-activesync-state.json"
  }

Minimal Outlook.com / Hotmail / Live account flow:

  ./tests/activesync-ms-device-oauth.py \
    --tenant consumers \
    --servertype personal-ms \
    --client-id [client ID] \
    --login [email@outlook.com] \
    --activesync-url https://eas.outlook.com/Microsoft-Server-ActiveSync \
    --token-json /tmp/libetpan-eas-token.json

  ./tests/activesync-sample \
    --server https://eas.outlook.com/Microsoft-Server-ActiveSync \
    --login [email@outlook.com] \
    --oauth-token "$(jq -r .access_token /tmp/libetpan-eas-token.json)" \
    --state-file /tmp/libetpan-activesync-state.json

Minimal Microsoft 365 work or school account flow:

  ./tests/activesync-ms-device-oauth.py \
    --tenant organizations \
    --servertype office365 \
    --client-id [client ID] \
    --login [email@example.com] \
    --activesync-url https://outlook.office365.com/Microsoft-Server-ActiveSync \
    --token-json /tmp/libetpan-eas-token.json

  ./tests/activesync-sample \
    --server https://outlook.office365.com/Microsoft-Server-ActiveSync \
    --login [email@example.com] \
    --oauth-token "$(jq -r .access_token /tmp/libetpan-eas-token.json)" \
    --state-file /tmp/libetpan-activesync-state.json

activesync-live-sync-test.sh
----------------------------
refreshes the cached OAuth token, probes the ActiveSync endpoint, then runs
activesync-sample against the live server.

It reuses tests/activesync-ms-oauth.local.json when present, otherwise it
defaults to:

  token JSON: /tmp/libetpan-eas-token.json
  state file: /tmp/libetpan-activesync-state.json
  server: https://eas.outlook.com/Microsoft-Server-ActiveSync

When the token JSON already contains OAuth metadata such as client_id, tenant,
oauth_version, servertype, scope, or resource, the wrapper passes those values
back to the OAuth helper for refresh.

Set LIBETPAN_ACTIVESYNC_LOGIN if the login is not present in the local config.

  LIBETPAN_ACTIVESYNC_LOGIN=email@outlook.com \
    ./tests/activesync-live-sync-test.sh

Equivalent one-off form:

  ./tests/activesync-live-sync-test.sh --login email@outlook.com

The email-only guarded suite runs all currently scripted live validation hooks.
It mutates only generated messages and temporary mail folders. Attachment
validation still requires at least one synced message with an attachment, and
ValidateCert is included only when certificate data is provided:

  ./tests/activesync-live-sync-test.sh --login email@outlook.com --email-suite

  ./tests/activesync-live-sync-test.sh --login email@outlook.com \
    --email-suite --validate-cert-file /tmp/certificate-base64.txt

Guarded live send validation is opt-in. It sends a generated plain-text
message to the authenticated account and saves a copy in Sent:

  LIBETPAN_ACTIVESYNC_SAMPLE_ARGS=--send-self-test \
    ./tests/activesync-live-sync-test.sh --login email@outlook.com

Guarded SmartReply and SmartForward validation are also opt-in. Each sends a
generated self-addressed source message, syncs Inbox until that source appears,
then replies to or forwards that generated item:

  LIBETPAN_ACTIVESYNC_SAMPLE_ARGS=--smart-reply-self-test \
    ./tests/activesync-live-sync-test.sh --login email@outlook.com

  LIBETPAN_ACTIVESYNC_SAMPLE_ARGS=--smart-forward-self-test \
    ./tests/activesync-live-sync-test.sh --login email@outlook.com

Guarded read-only recipient resolution validation is also opt-in:

  LIBETPAN_ACTIVESYNC_SAMPLE_ARGS=--resolve-self-test \
    ./tests/activesync-live-sync-test.sh --login email@outlook.com

Guarded recipient certificate lookup validation requests S/MIME certificate
metadata for the authenticated account:

  LIBETPAN_ACTIVESYNC_SAMPLE_ARGS=--resolve-cert-self-test \
    ./tests/activesync-live-sync-test.sh --login email@outlook.com

Guarded ValidateCert validation sends base64 certificate data from a local
file:

  LIBETPAN_ACTIVESYNC_SAMPLE_ARGS="--validate-cert-self-test \
    --validate-cert-file /tmp/certificate-base64.txt" \
    ./tests/activesync-live-sync-test.sh --login email@outlook.com

Guarded read-only mailbox search validation runs advertised Search/Find
commands against Inbox using the authenticated address as the query:

  LIBETPAN_ACTIVESYNC_SAMPLE_ARGS=--search-self-test \
    ./tests/activesync-live-sync-test.sh --login email@outlook.com

Guarded Ping validation runs a short heartbeat against the Inbox collection:

  LIBETPAN_ACTIVESYNC_SAMPLE_ARGS=--ping-self-test \
    ./tests/activesync-live-sync-test.sh --login email@outlook.com

Guarded Ping change validation sends a generated self-addressed message,
expects Ping to report Inbox as changed, then syncs that generated item:

  LIBETPAN_ACTIVESYNC_SAMPLE_ARGS=--ping-change-self-test \
    ./tests/activesync-live-sync-test.sh --login email@outlook.com

Guarded draft validation creates, updates, and deletes a generated draft in
the Drafts folder:

  LIBETPAN_ACTIVESYNC_SAMPLE_ARGS=--draft-self-test \
    ./tests/activesync-live-sync-test.sh --login email@outlook.com

Guarded multi-folder Sync validation syncs Inbox and Drafts together:

  LIBETPAN_ACTIVESYNC_SAMPLE_ARGS=--multi-sync-self-test \
    ./tests/activesync-live-sync-test.sh --login email@outlook.com

Guarded attachment validation fetches a byte range from the first attachment
FileReference seen during Sync:

  LIBETPAN_ACTIVESYNC_SAMPLE_ARGS=--attachment-self-test \
    ./tests/activesync-live-sync-test.sh --login email@outlook.com

Guarded generated mutation validation creates a generated draft, marks it
unread/read, flags/unflags it, then deletes it:

  LIBETPAN_ACTIVESYNC_SAMPLE_ARGS=--mutation-self-test \
    ./tests/activesync-live-sync-test.sh --login email@outlook.com

Guarded folder validation creates, verifies, renames, verifies, deletes, and
verifies a temporary mail folder with `FolderSync`:

  LIBETPAN_ACTIVESYNC_SAMPLE_ARGS=--folder-self-test \
    ./tests/activesync-live-sync-test.sh --login email@outlook.com

Guarded move validation creates a generated draft, creates a temporary mail
folder, moves the draft into that folder, then deletes the temporary folder:

  LIBETPAN_ACTIVESYNC_SAMPLE_ARGS=--move-self-test \
    ./tests/activesync-live-sync-test.sh --login email@outlook.com

ActiveSync command status matrix
--------------------------------

| Command / area | Current support | Mock coverage | Remaining gap |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| OPTIONS | Implemented. Parses protocol versions and advertised commands, caches advertised commands, and rejects non-advertised later commands. | Covered. | Live behavior still depends on server-advertised capability data. |
| Provision | Implemented policy request/ack subset with status and policy key parsing plus command/policy/result status helpers. | Covered. | Live validation remains. |
| Settings | Implemented DeviceInformation Set and UserInformation Get for email addresses/accounts plus command/result status helpers. | Covered. | Live validation remains. |
| FolderSync | Implemented hierarchy sync parsing for Add/Update/Delete. | Covered. | Live validation remains. |
| FolderCreate / FolderUpdate / FolderDelete | Implemented request/response shape with status, sync key, server id fields, invalid hierarchy sync-key detection, and folder resync helper. | Covered. A guarded `activesync-sample --folder-self-test` path exists for live create/update/delete plus FolderSync verification. | Live execution remains. |
| Sync server changes | Implemented single and multi-folder mail Sync with per-folder lookup/status helpers, per-folder MoreAvailable preservation, common options, server Add/Change/Delete parsing, empty responses, ConversationMode, Wait, HeartbeatInterval, ordered BodyPreference, AllOrNone, optional protected-mail rights-management support, and Supported property declarations. | Covered, including version guards for delayed controls and ConversationMode. A guarded `activesync-sample --multi-sync-self-test` path exists for live multi-folder validation. | Live execution remains. |
| Sync client commands | Implemented Add/Change/Delete/Fetch request encoding, Responses status/id/payload parsing, and command-response lookup/status helpers. | Covered. A guarded `activesync-sample --mutation-self-test` path exists for live generated-item mutation validation. | Live execution remains. |
| Read / flag / delete helpers | Implemented mark read/unread, flag/unflag, and delete helpers through Sync Change/Delete. | Covered. A guarded `activesync-sample --mutation-self-test` path exists for live generated-item validation. | Live execution remains. |
| Draft Add / Change | Implemented protocol 16.0/16.1 draft helpers with non-MIME body fields, MIME body-type validation, AirSyncBase body, and attachment Add request modeling. | Covered, including protocol and MIME field restriction guards. A guarded `activesync-sample --draft-self-test` path exists for live draft create/update/fetch/delete validation. | Live execution remains. |
| GetItemEstimate | Implemented single and multi-collection request/response modeling with per-collection status/estimate lookup helpers, Class, FilterType, and ConversationMode. | Covered. | Live interoperability remains. |
| ItemOperations Fetch | Implemented one-item fetch, repeated mailbox Fetch orchestration, attachment FileReference/Range fetch, BodyPartPreference request, BodyPart parsing, per-item status preservation, and truncation recovery predicates. | Covered, including BodyPartPreference protocol guards. A guarded `activesync-sample --attachment-self-test` path exists for live attachment range validation. | Live execution remains. |
| MoveItems | Implemented repeated moves, per-response source/status/destination-id parsing, request-context preservation, and protocol-specific status mapping. | Covered. A guarded `activesync-sample --move-self-test` path exists for live generated-item move validation. | Live execution remains. |
| ComposeMail | Implemented modern WBXML SendMail, SmartReply, and SmartForward, caller-supplied ClientId, SaveInSentItems, Mime, LongId, InstanceId, ReplaceMime, and legacy 2.5/12.x raw MIME bodies. | Covered. Guarded `activesync-sample --send-self-test`, `--smart-reply-self-test`, and `--smart-forward-self-test` paths exist for live generated-message validation. | Live execution remains. |
| Ping | Implemented heartbeat requests, changed folder parsing, empty-body cache, and correction statuses. | Covered. Guarded `activesync-sample --ping-self-test` and `--ping-change-self-test` paths exist for live heartbeat and controlled-change validation. | Live execution remains. |
| Search | Implemented legacy mailbox free-text/range helper and common email result parsing. | Covered, including protocol guards. A guarded `activesync-sample --search-self-test` path exists for live validation. | Live execution remains. |
| Find | Implemented protocol 16.1 mailbox free-text/range helper and result parsing. | Covered, including protocol guards. A guarded `activesync-sample --search-self-test` path exists for live validation when Find is advertised. | Live execution remains. |
| ResolveRecipients | Implemented repeated To, max ambiguous recipients, optional S/MIME certificate retrieval, command/per-input statuses, certificate data, response-status mapping, and certificate status helpers. | Covered. Guarded `activesync-sample --resolve-self-test` and `--resolve-cert-self-test` paths exist for live address and certificate-retrieval validation. | Live execution and certificate interoperability remain. |
| ValidateCert | Implemented repeated certificates, optional certificate chain, CRL-check request flag, command status, and per-certificate status parsing. | Covered. A guarded `activesync-sample --validate-cert-self-test --validate-cert-file PATH` path exists for live certificate validation. | Live execution remains. |
| Email data parsing | Implemented DisplayTo, Importance, ThreadTopic, InternetCPID, ContentClass, Categories, ConversationId, ConversationIndex, Email flag metadata, legacy Body/MIME data, size, and truncation parsing. | Covered. | Live validation remains. |
| Stubbed or absent public APIs | None in the current email-focused low-level API surface. | Bad-state coverage exists for public email-related APIs. | New API gaps are tracked as under-modeled areas rather than stubs. |

Notes:

ActiveSync Sync Add is not an IMAP-style append API. The protocol rejects
  client-created non-draft email items; draft synchronization is only supported
  by protocol versions 16.0 and 16.1. The draft helpers implement AirSyncBase
  body drafts, including MIME body-type drafts with the narrower spec-allowed
  field set. For normal outbound mail, use SendMail, SmartReply, or
  SmartForward.


gmail-google-oauth.py
---------------------
gets a Google OAuth access token with the device-code flow for local Gmail HTTP
testing. Token acquisition and refresh stay outside libEtPan; callers pass the
resulting bearer token to the low-level Gmail API.

By default the helper reads optional local config from
tests/gmail-google-oauth.local.json. The local config file is ignored by git
and can contain reusable values such as:

  {
    "client_id": "...apps.googleusercontent.com",
    "client_secret": "...",
    "scope": "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.readonly",
    "token_json": "/tmp/libetpan-gmail-token.json",
    "user": "me"
  }

Use a Google OAuth client for TVs and Limited Input devices. Some Google client
types require a client_secret at the token endpoint; include it only when your
client has one.

Minimal flow:

  ./tests/gmail-google-oauth.py \
    --client-id [client ID] \
    --token-json /tmp/libetpan-gmail-token.json \
    --probe-gmail

  ./tests/gmail-sample \
    --oauth-token "$(jq -r .access_token /tmp/libetpan-gmail-token.json)"

gmail-live-smoke-test.sh
------------------------
refreshes the cached Google OAuth token when client metadata is available,
probes the Gmail profile endpoint through the helper, then runs gmail-sample
against the live Gmail REST API.

It reuses tests/gmail-google-oauth.local.json when present, otherwise it
defaults to:

  token JSON: /tmp/libetpan-gmail-token.json
  user: me
  max results: 5

When no config or token JSON exists, the wrapper exits with status 77 to report
a skipped test.

  ./tests/gmail-live-smoke-test.sh

Equivalent one-off form:

  ./tests/gmail-live-smoke-test.sh \
    --config tests/gmail-google-oauth.local.json \
    --token-json /tmp/libetpan-gmail-token.json

The smoke test is read-only. It calls users.getProfile, labels.list, and
messages.list through libEtPan. Fetching metadata for the first listed message
is opt-in:

  ./tests/gmail-live-smoke-test.sh --fetch-first-message


jmap-live-smoke-test.sh
-----------------------
runs the low-level JMAP live-provider smoke sample when credentials are
available, and exits with status 77 to report a skipped test when they are not.

Read-only smoke test with a direct Session URL:

  JMAP_SESSION_URL=https://example.com/jmap/session \
  JMAP_ACCESS_TOKEN=[access token] \
    ./tests/jmap-live-smoke-test.sh

Read-only smoke test using .well-known discovery:

  JMAP_EMAIL=email@example.com \
  JMAP_ACCESS_TOKEN=[access token] \
    ./tests/jmap-live-smoke-test.sh

Guarded send validation is opt-in. It uploads/imports a generated RFC 5322
message and submits it only when JMAP_SEND_TO is set:

  JMAP_SESSION_URL=https://example.com/jmap/session \
  JMAP_ACCESS_TOKEN=[access token] \
  JMAP_SEND_TO=recipient@example.com \
    ./tests/jmap-live-smoke-test.sh


fastmail-jmap-oauth.py
----------------------
gets a Fastmail OAuth bearer token for local JMAP testing. Fastmail OAuth uses
authorization-code flow with PKCE, so this helper requires a Fastmail-registered
OAuth client id and a browser sign-in. For one-user testing, a Fastmail JMAP API
token can also be used directly as JMAP_ACCESS_TOKEN without this OAuth helper.

Read-only JMAP scopes:

  ./tests/fastmail-jmap-oauth.py user@example.com \
    --client-id=[registered client id] \
    --token-json=/tmp/fastmail-jmap-token.json \
    --env-file=/tmp/fastmail-jmap.env \
    --probe-jmap

Then run:

  . /tmp/fastmail-jmap.env
  ./tests/jmap-live-smoke-test.sh

The default scope is:

  urn:ietf:params:jmap:core urn:ietf:params:jmap:mail

Add submission only when testing send behavior:

  ./tests/fastmail-jmap-oauth.py user@example.com \
    --client-id=[registered client id] \
    --scope="urn:ietf:params:jmap:core urn:ietf:params:jmap:mail urn:ietf:params:jmap:submission"


mime-parse
----------
parse a MIME message and show the structure

syntax: mime-parse emailfile.eml


mime-create
-----------
create a message and show the resulting RFC 2822 format

syntax: mime-create


compose-msg
-----------

creates a RFC 2822 message with MIME parts

syntax: compose-msg "text" filename



all the following programs will take as argument :

  --driver=(pop3|imap|nntp|mbox|mh)  -d pop3 (pop3|imap|nntp|mbox|mh)

  default driver is mbox

  --server={server-name} -s {server-name}
  --port={port-number}   -p {port-number}
  --tls                  -t
  --starttls             -x
  --user={login}         -u {login}
  --password={password}  -v {password}
  --path={mailbox}       -l {mailbox}
  --apop                 -a
  --cache={directory}    -c {directory}

the default driver is mbox with the path /var/mail/$USER

frm-simple, frm, frm-tree
-------------------------

frm-simple will list all the mails of a mailbox without any MIME decoding.

frm will list all the mails of a mailbox and will decode the fields.

frm-tree will do the same thing as frm and will also show the threads
  of the folder.


fetch-attachment
----------------

  fetch-attachment gets all the named attachment of the given message
of the chosen mailbox and writes them on the current directory.

  The program should be given message numbers (as given by frm) as
additionnal arguments.


readmsg-simple, readmsg
-----------------------

  readmsg-simple will display the content of the given messages.
All the content (headers and body) will be displayed.

  readmsg will display only the text parts of the given messages.

  The program should be given message numbers (as given by frm) as
additionnal arguments.
